{
  "_comment": "Canonical registry of localised URL slugs. The key of each entry in `routes` is the English route, which stays the internal identifier everywhere in the build; the values are the path segment actually published under each locale prefix. A locale that omits a route keeps the English slug.",
  "_script_policy": "Only Latin-script locales get translated slugs. Cyrillic, Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, Bengali, Thai and CJK locales keep the English slug on purpose: a native-script path is percent-encoded the moment it leaves the address bar, and this site's readers paste URLs into tickets, runbooks and payment-scheme correspondence where an unreadable %D0%BF%D0%BE... is worse than an English word. This is a reversible choice — adding a native slug later is a registry edit plus one more redirect stub. See DECISIONS.md D-005.",
  "_invariants": [
    "Every slug is lowercase ASCII letters, digits and hyphens. Diacritics are folded (é to e, ł to l, ğ to g) so the URL survives copy-paste through systems that mangle UTF-8.",
    "Slugs are unique within a locale. Two routes colliding would silently overwrite one page.",
    "Message-type routes (pacs.008.001.13 and siblings) are never translated. They are ISO 20022 identifiers, not words.",
    "A slug, once published, is permanent. Changing one requires a new redirect stub, never an edit in place."
  ],
